--Description:
-- Goal: obtain 2016 state-level counts of crashes that involved at least one
--       distracted driver, excluding the three “non-distracted” categories
--       (‘Not Distracted’, ‘Unknown if Distracted’, ‘Not Reported’), and
--       display readable state names.
--
-- Key facts I checked:
--   • `distract_2016`  → has state_number & distraction info but NO state_name.
--   • `drimpair_2016`  → also lacks state_name (that’s why the earlier join failed).
--   • `drimpair_2015`  → DOES contain both state_number and state_name.
--
-- Strategy (thinking process):
--   1. Build a small lookup (CTE `state_lookup`) using DISTINCT pairs
--      (state_number, state_name) from `drimpair_2015`.
--   2. Join that lookup to the 2016 distraction records on state_number.
--   3. Filter out the three unwanted distraction labels.
--   4. Count DISTINCT crashes (`consecutive_number`) for each state
--      (multiple distracted drivers in the same crash still count once).
--   5. Order by crash count and return the 20 states with the highest totals.
WITH state_lookup AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT state_number, state_name
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.drimpair_2015`
)
SELECT
  d.state_number,
  l.state_name,
  COUNT(DISTINCT d.consecutive_number) AS accidents_2016
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.distract_2016` AS d
